**Read:** [Systems Engineering Handbook — NASA](../../../engineering/mechanical-engineering-and-machine-design/sources/Systems-Engineering-Handbook-NASA.lingua#r_7ZQFZ9E1NAVG27SE2R5S24PDBQ), chapter 5; the facility studies and surface-method validation; [Sampling contributions, thresholds, and results near zero](../../../inquiry/metrology/uncertainty/Sampling-and-censoring.lingua#r_3RRJRMNNAPMVYJ9Z6VGY2DV0CG).

Design a paper qualification for a benign optical-assembly enclosure. Define the useful function, relevant airborne and surface quantities, product exposure and operating domain. Map each claim to inspection, analysis or an observation that can challenge it.

Use the existing airflow, transient, sampling and surface models to generate a consistent hypothetical system. Include one interaction and one localized condition that a room-wide average misses. Define the independent sampling units, instrument response and reference uncertainties.

Compare a correctly built system, an interface error and a system that meets a facility quantity while failing the product's functional requirement. Explain which observations distinguish the cases. Keep the configuration and the supported domain with every conclusion.

**Result to explain:** A defensible qualification argument with independently checked calculations, uncertainty and explicit limits. State the unresolved evidence needed for a real installation and how one subsequent change would affect the argument.

**Return:** [Clean manufacturing and contamination control — from familiar objects to controlled processes](../Clean-manufacturing-and-contamination-control.lingua#r_7D8SQ8BVQDZQBDBGC6DP0JAMZN).
